course: dictionary
lesson: 14844
title: Old words: raiment
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Raiment is an old word for clothes, especially fine clothes. You will meet it in the Bible and in old poems and stories. Today people say «clothes» instead.
explain: «Take no thought for your life... nor yet for your body, what ye shall put on. Is not the life more than meat, and the body than raiment?» Here raiment means clothing. The sentence asks whether the body matters more than what we wear. Ye means you, and meat means food: these are old words too.
explain: Use context to check. «The king put on his fine raiment for the feast.» What would fit in place of raiment? Clothes. Swap in clothes and the sentence still makes sense, so raiment means clothes.
fact: Raiment means clothing, especially fine clothes.
fact: Raiment is an old word, found mostly in the Bible and in old poems and stories.
fact: Raiment is uncountable: you do not say "a raiment" or "raiments", just as you do not say "a clothing".
fact: Today we say «clothes» for the same idea.
fact: A good check is to swap in a modern word, like clothes, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The queen wore raiment of gold and silver to the feast.» What does raiment mean? | *Clothes, especially fine ones | Food | Shoes | Jewels
exercise: «Is not the life more than meat, and the body than raiment?» Find and copy the word in this line that means clothing. | *raiment | meat | body | life
exercise: An old poem says, «Her raiment shone in the moonlight.» What would we say today instead of raiment? | Moonlight | Rain | Raiders | *Clothes
test: «Her raiment rustled as she walked down the stone steps.» What is raiment in this sentence? | *Her clothes | A hat | A basket | Her hair
test: Mia writes "many raiments" because she thinks raiment is a countable noun. What is the correct version? | "Many raiments" is correct, because every noun has a plural | "Many clothes" is wrong, so she should write "many raiment" | *No change: raiment is uncountable, so it has no plural | "Many raiment" is wrong, so she should write "many raimentes"
exam: Which modern word would best replace raiment in «He chose his raiment carefully for the wedding»? | Weather | Tree | *Clothes | Music
